        <zone h='6154' id='16' name='Trend (Qty)' show-title='false' w='7632' x='44789' y='9615' />
        <zone h='6154' id='17' name='Trend (Sales)' show-title='false' w='7632' x='57579' y='9615' />
        <zone h='6154' id='18' name='Trend (Profit)' show-title='false' w='7632' x='70263' y='9462' />
        <zone h='6154' id='19' name='Trend (Discount)' show-title='false' w='7632' x='83053' y='9154' />
        <zone _.fcp.ExternalImageContentForZones.true...image-file-url='https://www.talabat.com/assets/images/logo.svg' h='2231' id='20' is-scaled='1' type-v2='bitmap' w='8526' x='28579' y='1231' />
        <zone h='4000' id='22' mode='compact' param='[Parameters].[Parameter 5]' type-v2='paramctrl' w='8737' x='81947' y='17308' />
        <zone h='4077' id='23' mode='compact' param='[Parameters].[Parameter 6]' type-v2='paramctrl' w='9105' x='72105' y='17308' />
        <zone h='3846' id='27' mode='compact' param='[Parameters].[Parameter 7]' type-v2='paramctrl' w='7632' x='82895' y='38923' />
        <zone forceUpdate='true' h='9154' id='39' type-v2='text' w='23053' x='1474' y='4692'>
          <formatted-text>
            <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue' fontsize='12'>1. Dashboard title</run>
            <run>Æ&#10;</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue'>The dashboard title is positioned at the top, accompanied by the logo on the left-hand side. The text of the dashboard is set in white with a size of 20 and has an orange background. You can access the Talabat logo by clicking the following link: </run>
            <run auto-url='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue' hyperlink='tabdoc:load-url url=&amp;quot;https://www.talabat.com/assets/images/logo.svg.&amp;quot;'>https://www.talabat.com/assets/images/logo.svg.</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue'> For aesthetic consistency, it is recommended to maintain a preferable height of 50 for the title section.</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Æ </run>
          </formatted-text>
          <zone-style>
            <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
            <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
            <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
            <format attr='background-color' value='#e6e6e6' />
          </zone-style>
        </zone>
        <zone forceUpdate='true' h='11077' id='45' type-v2='text' w='23105' x='1421' y='14615'>
          <formatted-text>
            <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue' fontsize='12'>2. Main KPIs Section</run>
            <run>Æ&#10;</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue'>We typically begin building the dashboard with the main Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) view, as it serves as the primary section that users will explore, helping them grasp the overall performance at a glance and guiding their focus towards subsequent insights. Careful selection of relevant metrics is crucial, and it should include comparisons such as percentage growth against previous periods, benchmarks, or targets to provide valuable context. Optionally you can also include a small trend view just to highlight any anomalies. </run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Æ </run>
          </formatted-text>
          <zone-style>
            <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
            <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
            <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
            <format attr='background-color' value='#e6e6e6' />
          </zone-style>
        </zone>
        <zone forceUpdate='true' h='22846' id='46' type-v2='text' w='23000' x='1474' y='26385'>
          <formatted-text>
            <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue' fontsize='12'>3. Metrics Breakdown</run>
            <run>Æ&#10;</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue'>After selecting your main KPIs, the next step is to break down these metrics using relevant dimensions, such as country, city, category, or segment. This approach enables viewers to analyze the data from multiple angles, identify anomalies within specific dimensions, and compare the performance of each dimension. For this purpose, various chart types can be employed, such as </run>
            <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue'>vertical bar</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue'> charts showing values for each dimension with a percentage of growth or performance against a target or benchmark. Additionally, </run>
            <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue'>pie charts</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue'> can effectively showcase the main dimension&apos;s share (limited to 3 or 4 dimension values), while </run>
            <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue'>donut charts</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue'> are ideal for displaying ratios or conversion rates. </run>
            <run>Æ&#10;</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue'>You can be creative building and selecting the chart types in this section of the dashboard. make sure to choose simple and straightforward charts when building a dashboard for a wide audience. </run>
            <run>Æ&#10;</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue'>To maintain a clean and uncluttered dashboard, consider utilizing parameters, which allow users to add multiple metrics in one view and then filter and switch between them as needed. This approach enhances the dashboard&apos;s flexibility and empowers users to explore the data according to their specific needs and preferences.</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Æ </run>
          </formatted-text>
          <zone-style>
            <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
            <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
            <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
            <format attr='background-color' value='#e6e6e6' />
          </zone-style>
        </zone>
        <zone h='3154' id='47' type-v2='text' w='23000' x='1474' y='923'>
          <formatted-text>
            <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue' fontsize='14'>Dashboard template design:</run>
          </formatted-text>
          <zone-style>
            <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
            <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
            <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
            <format attr='background-color' value='#f5eddc' />
          </zone-style>
        </zone>
        <zone forceUpdate='true' h='18077' id='51' type-v2='text' w='23000' x='1474' y='50077'>
          <formatted-text>
            <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue' fontsize='12'>4. Historical trends</run>
            <run>Æ&#10;</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue'>Historical trends offer valuable context and a broader perspective on the evolution of metrics over time, aiding viewers in identifying long-term patterns and seasonal variations. Additionally, they prove essential for forecasting, prediction, and anomaly detection.</run>
            <run>Æ&#10;</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue'>Typically, historical trends are presented in a </run>
            <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue'>bar chart</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue'> format, and an overlaying </run>
            <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue'>line charts</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue'> to facilitate easy comparison. Also the ability to switch between different date parts, such as day, week, or month, based on their preference.</run>
            <run>Æ&#10;</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue'>To ensure a user-friendly design, keep it simple and default the date range to a maximum of 90 days, depending on the available space. For added flexibility, consider making overlaying metrics optional using a parameter selection feature. This way, users can choose whether to view multiple metrics overlaid on the same chart for deeper insights.</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Æ </run>
          </formatted-text>
          <zone-style>
            <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
            <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
            <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
            <format attr='background-color' value='#e6e6e6' />
          </zone-style>
        </zone>
        <zone h='13077' id='54' type-v2='text' w='22947' x='1526' y='69231'>
          <formatted-text>
            <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue' fontsize='12'>5. Detailed Table Views</run>
            <run>Æ&#10;</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue'>To optimize dashboard performance, it&apos;s better to avoid highly granular table views as they can often lead to performance issues and slowdown the dashboard. However, for operational dashboards, including tables that display lists of countries, cities, or vendor information is acceptable. It&apos;s essential to strike a balance by avoiding item level, session, customer, or order level data, which can potentially impact the dashboard&apos;s efficiency, we can utilize other tools like Looker for that (contact the reporting team for support).</run>
            <run>Æ&#10;</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue'>You can also improve table charts performance by adding top n filter and watch out for the numbers of marks used. </run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Æ </run>
          </formatted-text>
          <zone-style>
            <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
            <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
            <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
            <format attr='background-color' value='#e6e6e6' />
          </zone-style>
        </zone>
        <zone h='14077' id='55' type-v2='text' w='22895' x='1579' y='83000'>
          <formatted-text>
            <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue' fontsize='12'>6. Dashboard Filters</run>
            <run>Æ&#10;</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue'>Lastly, ensure you separate your global filters that controls the entire dashboard from view-specific filters for better organization. Place global filters vertically on the right section of the dashboard to accommodate numerous filters effectively. On the other hand, position view-specific filters next to the view title.</run>
            <run>Æ&#10;</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Helvetica Neue'>To enhance user experience, consider adding an &quot;Apply&quot; button for filters that accept multiple values. This helps users control when the filter takes effect, providing them with a clear understanding of their selections. Additionally, selecting &quot;Only Relevant Values&quot; for filters can streamline the user&apos;s choices, presenting only the applicable options, thereby improving usability and reducing clutter.</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book'>Æ </run>
          </formatted-text>
          <zone-style>
            <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
            <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
            <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
            <format attr='background-color' value='#e6e6e6' />
          </zone-style>
        </zone>
      </zones>
